 "brief": {
  "question": "What does the parliamentary record show about climate & energy?",
  "answer": "The strongest finding in the record is a stark partisan divide over whether the Howard government\u2019s climate and energy record was one of achievement or broken promise. Government speakers claimed a \u201cAAA record\u201d built on a $1 billion greenhouse response, the world\u2019s only dedicated Australian Greenhouse Office, a $400 million Greenhouse Gas Abatement Program, and a Mandatory Renewable Energy Target (MRET) requiring an extra 9,500 gigawatt hours of renewable electricity by 2010\u2014described as equivalent to two Snowy Mountains schemes. They also claimed current measures would cut emissions by about 60 million tonnes a year in the 2008-12 Kyoto period, equal to removing all passenger cars from Australian roads. The government refused to ratify the Kyoto protocol, arguing that even full compliance would cut global greenhouse concentrations by less than one per cent, that major emitters including developing countries were excluded, and that long-term costs outweighed short-term benefits\u2014citing premiers of Queensland and Western Australia who said their resource states would be disadvantaged.\n\nOpposition speakers consistently challenged those claims, arguing the government was doing the minimum and breaking its own commitments. Labor called for ratification and a five per cent MRET rather than the two per cent target, citing Origin Energy research that a five per cent target would cut emissions by one per cent with negligible electricity cost increases. They pointed to actual electricity demand growth of 3.8 per cent a year from 1996 to 2000, far above the 2.1 per cent assumption used to set the MRET, meaning renewables would struggle to maintain their existing market share. Labor also reported that the Australian Greenhouse Office had been cut by $30 million, or 45 per cent, between 2001-03, and that the $400 million Greenhouse Gas Abatement Program had delivered less than $129 million, with the Bureau of Transport and Regional Economics projecting transport emissions would reach 47 per cent above 1990 levels by 2010 and 68 per cent above by 2020.\n\nThe debate shifted noticeably between 2000 and 2003. In 2000, Labor reserved its final position on the Renewable Energy (Electricity) Bill, complained it was rushed, questioned whether a technology-neutral subsidy would simply favour biomass, and noted the government\u2019s own cost estimate of around"
 },
