  {
   "question": "What has been said in parliament about coal mining and the transition to renewable energy?",
   "answer": "Based on the parliamentary debate in the context, several views were expressed about coal mining and the transition to renewable energy:\n\n- Many speakers acknowledged the coal industry\u2019s importance to Australia, particularly for exports, jobs, and electricity generation. For example, it was described as Australia\u2019s largest commodity export, a key source of low-cost electricity, and vital to regions like the Hunter and electorates such as Charlton, Paterson, and McMillan.\n- Some speakers defended coal and opposed rapid moves away from it, arguing that the Kyoto protocol would harm the coal industry and that \u201cclean coal\u201d technology and coal seam methane should be considered, though others called clean coal a \u201ctransparent fraud\u201d that would not reduce carbon dioxide emissions.\n- Several speakers supported the transition to renewable energy and criticized the government\u2019s renewable energy target as too low. They argued for increasing the mandatory renewable energy target from two per cent to five per cent, and some mentioned Greenpeace\u2019s call for 10 per cent.\n- The debate also included discussion of the Renewable Energy (Electricity) Amendment Bill 2002, which aimed to improve the administration of the existing renewable energy legislation. Some speakers said the bill did not go far enough and that Australia should ratify the Kyoto protocol and invest more in renewable energy, while others supported the government\u2019s approach, including not ratifying Kyoto and instead focusing on greenhouse gas abatement programs.\n- There was also reference to the history of the Joint Coal Board, coalmining safety, miners\u2019 deaths, and the need to protect workers\u2019 entitlements and communities, as well as the transfer of coal industry responsibilities from the Commonwealth to New South Wales.\n\nOverall, the context shows a divided parliament: one side emphasized the economic importance of coal and cautious, technology-based approaches to emissions; the other side argued for stronger and faster action on renewable energy and greenhouse gas reductions.",
